<em><a href="https://www.macworld.com/article/3209936/ios-27-beta-confirms-apples-entire-iphone-18-roadmap-with-6-new-models.html">Macworld</a></em> reports that iOS 27 beta 5, released to developers on <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/08/10/apple-seeds-ios-27-beta-5/">Monday</a>, contains hidden code revealing six unreleased iPhone models – although you've probably heard of them before.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2025/09/better-iphone-17-lineup.jpg" alt="" width="1117" height="629" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1018042" /><br/>
The following references are said to have been found alongside references to existing iPhone models, in system files associated with the iPhone's battery drivers and Battery Intelligence features:<br/>
<ul><br/>
  <li><strong>V62</strong> — iPhone Air 2</li><br/>
  <li><strong>V63</strong> — iPhone 18 Pro</li><br/>
  <li><strong>V64</strong> — iPhone 18 Pro Max</li><br/>
  <li><strong>V67</strong> — iPhone 18</li><br/>
  <li><strong>V68</strong> — iPhone Ultra</li><br/>
  <li><strong>V69</strong> — iPhone 18e</li><br/>
</ul>It's the first time the full iPhone 18 lineup has been mentioned in code found in an iOS 27 beta. To be clear, the discovered references are the codes in bold, excluding the names (which are inferred) but these internal Apple identifiers have appeared in <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/12/15/apple-leak-unreleased-devices-codenames/">previous leaks</a> or have been used by <em>Bloomberg</em>'s Mark Gurman in his reporting, allowing them to be matched to the expected iPhone models.  <br/>
<br/>
Apple has not publicly confirmed the existence of any upcoming iPhone 18 models, but regular readers will know that they have been heavily rumored for several months now. <br/>
<br/>
